<article class="env-lemma" id="08XV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08XV">Lemma <span data-tag="08XV">47.3.7</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian ring. A direct sum of injective modules is injective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $E_ i$ be a family of injective modules parametrized by a set $I$. Set $E = \bigoplus E_ i$. To show that $E$ is injective we use Injectives, Lemma <a href="/tag/05NU" data-tag="05NU">19.2.6</a>. Thus let $\varphi : J \to E$ be a module map from an ideal of $R$ into $E$. As $J$ is a finite $R$-module (because $R$ is Noetherian) we can find finitely many elements $i_1, \ldots , i_ r \in I$ such that $\varphi $ maps into $\bigoplus _{j = 1, \ldots , r} E_{i_ j}$. Then we can extend $\varphi $ into $\bigoplus _{j = 1, \ldots , r} E_{i_ j}$ using the injectivity of the modules $E_{i_ j}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>